  "content": {
    "id": "p40-q64-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "17",
    "prompt": "Let $x=\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{1+\\cdots}}}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p40-q64-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $x=\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x^2-x-1=0$ and $x>0$, so $x=\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$.",
          "worked": "Interpret the infinite radical as the limit of its usual positive finite truncations. Its repeated tail has value $x$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\sqrt{1+x}\\\\x^2&=1+x\\\\x^2-x-1&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{1\\pm\\sqrt5}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $x>0$, $x=\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q64-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Deduce that $\\left(\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{7+3\\sqrt5}{2}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{7+3\\sqrt5}{2}$.\n\nUse $x^2=x+1$ to obtain $x^4=3x+2$.",
          "worked": "Using $x^2=x+1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x^4&=(x+1)^2\\\\x^4&=x^2+2x+1\\\\x^4&=(x+1)+2x+1\\\\x^4&=3x+2\\\\x^4&=3\\left(\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}\\right)+2\\\\x^4&=\\frac{7+3\\sqrt5}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $\\left(\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{7+3\\sqrt5}{2}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 66
      }
    ]
  },
